<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sobekneferu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1777–1773 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a> · Also known as Sobekneferure</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sobekneferu was the last ruler of the Twelfth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning for nearly four years in the late nineteenth century BCE. Probably a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, she succeeded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv">Amenemhat IV</a> and is the first woman known for certain to have taken the full royal titles of a king. Her death ended the dynasty and opened the Thirteenth Dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sobekneferu.jpg" alt="Sobekneferu" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hedwig Fechheimer (1871-1942), Die Plastik der Ägypter, Berlin 1914, pl. 57 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Sobekneferu_(Berlin_Egyptian_Museum_14475).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sobekneferu, whose name means Beauty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobek">Sobek</a>, is usually presumed to have been a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, though the link between him and his two successors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv">Amenemhat IV</a> and Sobekneferu, is uncertain. Her mother is unknown, and either of Amenemhat III's two known wives, Aat and Khenemetneferhedjet III, is a candidate. The ancient historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> states that Amenemhat IV and she were brother and sister, and there is speculation about a marriage between them, but no contemporary evidence for it.</p><p>The reasons for her accession are obscure. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv">Amenemhat IV</a> may have left no male heir, or there may have been a dispute over legitimacy. She never associated herself with Amenemhat IV and asserted her legitimacy instead through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>. She adopted the full royal titulary, modifying the traditional titles to mark her womanhood, as with daughter of Re and female <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>, and was the first ruler to carry the name of the crocodile god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobek">Sobek</a> in both her birth name and her throne name, Sobekkare.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Turin Canon credits her with a reign of three years, ten months and 24 days. Contemporary evidence is scant: a few partial statues, seals and inscriptions, mostly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a>, where she is credited with completing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labyrinth">Labyrinth</a>, the mortuary temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>'s pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawara">Hawara</a>. A graffito at the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumma">Kumma</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> records the height of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> inundation in her third regnal year, which shows that she kept authority across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><p>Her funerary monument has not been identified. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> from Harageh mentions a site called Sekhem Sobekneferu that may refer to it, and a storeroom administrator named on a stela is evidence of an active funerary cult.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Her death brought the Twelfth Dynasty and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a> to a close and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>. The transition seems to have been smooth, as there is no evidence of a violent collapse. The early Thirteenth Dynasty was marked by a rapid succession of short-lived rulers, the first of whom is debated, with Wegaf and Sobekhotep I the main candidates. Sobekhotep I appears to have modelled his throne name on her Nebty name, which suggests that he sought legitimacy from her and held her in esteem.</p><p>Later king lists record her. She appears on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> list of the Thutmosid period and on the Saqqara Tablet and the Turin Canon of the Ramesside period, and in the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a>. The Abydos King List omits her, along with all other female kings and the rulers of the intermediate periods and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Amarna</a> period, which shows whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> regarded as legitimate rulers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sobekneferu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Twelfth Dynasty ended with Amenemhat IV and then <b>Sobekneferu</b>, the first woman known for certain to have taken the full titles of a king, around 1773 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
